# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# ...except the template. `.env*` was swallowing it, so the one file that tells
# a new developer (or a deploy) which variables exist was untracked — while
# `.env.example` itself is referenced by the README and the runbook. It holds
# names and placeholders only, never real values.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Prisma generated client
/src/generated/prisma

# Uploaded user/group images
/public/storage/

# Documentation IS part of this repo.
#
# A global ignore on this machine (`core.excludesFile`, `*.md`) was silently
# keeping RUNBOOK.md, HOW-IT-WORKS.md and src/server/ARCHITECTURE.md out of
# version control — only README/AGENTS/CLAUDE.md had been force-added. That is
# almost certainly why those three drifted so far from the code: a file that
# cannot appear in a diff never gets reviewed alongside the change that
# invalidates it.
#
# A repo-level .gitignore takes precedence over core.excludesFile, so this
# re-includes them here without touching the machine-wide config (which is a
# reasonable default for scratch directories — just not for a repo whose
# architecture notes are the authority on how the backend works).
#
# node_modules is excluded above, and git does not descend into an ignored
# directory, so this does not pull in dependency documentation.
!*.md
